WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:03.100
"The terror, which would not end

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:07.030
for another twenty-eight
years--if it ever did end--

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.630
began, so far as I know or can tell,

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:13.770
with a boat made from
a sheet of newspapers

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:17.570
floating down a gutter swollen with rain."

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.050
This is how Stephen King begins "It,"

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:24.170
his 22nd novel, published in 1986.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:28.430
It's a passage that really
exemplifies what King does best:

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:32.680
instantly engrossing you in
the horrific and unknown.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.180
And it's this unique
ability that has made him

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.640
one of the best-selling
writers of our time,

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:41.800
selling over 350 million copies worldwide

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:46.170
and earning him the distinguished
title of Master of Horror.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.760
Yet, despite his success and popularity,

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.560
King is still a difficult
writer to discuss

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.370
among the literary circle.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.265
Once described by The New York Times as

00:00:55.265 --> 00:00:59.350
"a writer of fairly engaging
and preposterous claptrap,"

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:01.133
there has been a long-standing discussion

00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:04.233
on whether King's works
are really literature

00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:06.608
or just glorified pulp.

00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:08.692
Nonetheless, King has managed to create

00:01:08.692 --> 00:01:11.691
some of the most iconic
and haunting stories

00:01:11.691 --> 00:01:13.610
ever to come out of the genre.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.100
Which raises the question:

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.293
How does he do it?

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.130
Like any writer, King isn't shy

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.320
to share his sources of inspiration,

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.790
most notably writers like
Richard Matheson of "I Am Legend"

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:36.200
and Bram Stoker of the
now classic "Dracula."

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.330
But perhaps the writer that most closely

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:41.841
resembles his style is H.P. Lovecraft,

00:01:41.841 --> 00:01:45.640
one of the most influential
writers of the horror genre.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.500
He single-handedly created
a whole new subgenre

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.670
that is now more commonly
known as cosmic horror,

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:53.900
in which unknown cosmic entities

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.793
and phenomena beyond our understanding,

00:01:56.793 --> 00:02:00.068
often portrayed as
ancient, mythical monsters,

00:02:00.068 --> 00:02:02.485
became the subject of horror.

00:02:02.485 --> 00:02:06.460
But the monsters of Lovecraft
were never really monsters.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.699
Instead, they were
metaphors that symbolized

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:11.980
Lovecraft's deep fear of
the rapid technological

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:16.130
and scientific advancements
in the early 20th century.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:19.190
The helplessness he felt
towards the changes around him

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.860
reflected in the hopeless
struggle of people

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.683
against forces that are
far beyond their control.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.810
Lovecraft believed that people's inability

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.143
to truly understand their reality

00:02:30.143 --> 00:02:33.092
was the most merciful thing in the world

00:02:33.092 --> 00:02:34.058
and that doing so

00:02:34.058 --> 00:02:36.991
would be enough to drive
anyone to insanity.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.183
In many ways, this is
the same kind of horror

00:02:40.183 --> 00:02:42.130
that King taps into.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:46.320
Whether it's vampires, a
haunted hotel, or a rabid dog,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.758
the subjects of King's horror

00:02:47.758 --> 00:02:51.140
all represent our fear for something else.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.585
More specifically, the societal fears

00:02:53.585 --> 00:02:55.580
of the American people.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.870
In "Danse Macabre," King's
study of the horror genre,

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.760
he explains that there are
two different kinds of horror.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.470
The first is horror that plays

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.550
on our phobic pressure points.

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:09.690
These are fears based on
our individual phobias,

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:13.280
like the fear of spiders or ghosts,

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.010
something that a lot of recent

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.570
horror movies have based themselves on.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:19.970
But these horrors also have a clear limit,

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.180
as they target a very specific group

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.220
of people with that specific phobia.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:28.790
Which is why more effective
and successful works of horror

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:32.300
play with what's known as
national pressure points.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:35.720
These are political, societal,
and psychological fears

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:39.390
that are shared by a
wider spectrum of people.

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.670
Like in the works of Lovecraft and King,

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:46.130
are represented by the
abnormal and the supernatural.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.260
It's what King excels at,

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.440
living through some of the most

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.570
tumultuous periods in American history.

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:54.810
His debut novel, "Carrie," on the surface

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.600
a book about a girl
with telekinetic powers,

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.860
is really a story about the suppression

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.280
of female sexuality in the '60s,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:03.930
published just six years after the

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:07.840
famous Miss America protest in 1968.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.340
And in "The Shining," a book about

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.970
a family stranded in a haunted hotel,

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.640
King picks apart the concept of patriarchy

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.810
that is deeply rooted
into the American culture

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:21.920
to discuss the cyclical
nature of parental legacy.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.930
Sometimes it's more personal.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.620
Two of his most famous
works, "Misery" and "Cujo,"

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:28.650
deal with addiction and the lack

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:30.650
of self-control it accompanies

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:32.270
and were written by King during his

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.726
own struggle with drugs and alcohol.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:38.150
Like Lovecraft, the
horrors that King portrays

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:41.801
are self-reflective and
grounded in our reality.

00:04:41.801 --> 00:04:43.667
Mrs. Carmody: And it is
his fault, yes, it is,

00:04:43.667 --> 00:04:44.705
Pvt. Jessup: No!

00:04:44.705 --> 00:04:46.240
It is not my fault!

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:47.580
Narrator: Which in turn makes

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.793
the horror feel that much more real.

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:54.240
They're representations of American fear.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.690
Things that threaten the very foundation

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:58.870
of the society we live in.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:02.840
And just like Lovecraft, it's
reality that terrifies King.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:04.840
As he once put it, it isn't the physical

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.270
or mental abnormalities
that really horrify us,

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:11.763
but the lack of order in
our reality they represent.

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:15.533
So, how does King put this on paper?

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:20.490
The first thing to note is
that situation comes first.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:22.950
King's books are often based on situations

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.020
rather than intricate plots.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.870
Every single one of his works is based

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.010
on a series of what-if scenarios.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:32.360
Like what if an ancient,
cosmic evil in the shape

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.890
of a clown terrorized
a small town in Maine?

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:38.820
Then he drops a group of
well-fleshed-out characters

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.130
right into the heart of the situation.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:43.673
But it's not to help
them work their way free,

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:47.700
but to simply watch what happens.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.380
And it's this spontaneity
that allows for some of

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:54.518
the most unexpected and shocking
moments in King's stories.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:56.900
And it's impossible to talk about

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:00.140
Stephen King without
talking about suspense.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:01.360
As he said himself,

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.136
Stephen King: Well, if
you ask the ordinary,

00:06:06.136 --> 00:06:07.762
run-of-the-mill reader,

00:06:07.762 --> 00:06:10.440
if there is such a guy or such a gal,

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:14.120
I guess that I write horror novels.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:18.010
I think what I actually
write are suspense novels.

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:20.940
Narrator: Another master of
suspense, Alfred Hitchcock,

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.030
once said suspense is
"the most powerful means

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:26.280
of holding on to the viewer's attention."

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.640
And it's what King also excels at.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.440
Grabbing your attention and keeping

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.090
it on something horrific,

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:34.490
so you're unable to turn away.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:36.940
And he accomplishes this by applying three

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:39.710
of the most basic literary devices.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:42.050
The first step is foreshadowing.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:44.390
Both his books and adaptations are riddled

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:47.718
with lines and moments that
hint at what's to come.

00:06:47.718 --> 00:06:48.943
Bill: Georgie?

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:55.210
Be careful.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:56.500
Narrator: This is why King's stories

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:00.260
have an overarching
sense of doom and dread.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.270
Unlike most horror novels and films,

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:06.353
it's not the uncertainty of
danger that has you on edge,

00:07:06.353 --> 00:07:10.303
but exactly when that
danger will finally happen.

00:07:10.303 --> 00:07:12.380
Then, the next step is callback,

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:15.240
where King racks up the
suspense by continuously

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:19.030
reminding the audience
that the danger is lurking.

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:20.640
And oftentimes, it takes a while

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.340
for the danger to actually show its face.

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:25.700
And then, finally, the payoff.

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:28.528
In which the suspense
we've been building towards

00:07:28.528 --> 00:07:31.180
reaches its peak and we finally

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:33.700
face the danger we've been waiting for.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:38.550
And it's at this final
moment that King scares us.

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:41.610
And you can very well see
this throughout King's career.

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:44.510
Even in the passage
that started this video.

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:46.226
With just one line,

00:07:46.226 --> 00:07:48.526
foreshadowing the terror to come,

00:07:48.526 --> 00:07:51.220
a callback to the
longevity of said terror,

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:53.800
and, finally, the payoff to the very thing

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:55.363
that started it all.

00:07:56.290 --> 00:08:00.220
Analyzing the works of Stephen
King is a great opportunity

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:03.910
to understand what really
scares us at the end of the day.

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:07.400
His works are prime examples
that show us that our object

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:10.833
of fear very much exists
in our own reality.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:15.700
Sometimes it's not the monsters
or ghosts that scare us,

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:18.380
but the horrors of everyday life

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:22.100
lurking in the corners, waiting to strike.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:24.400
And it's this horror that King knows

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:28.270
and understands perhaps
better than anyone.

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:31.940
And if that's not enough to
make him the Master of Horror,

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:36.507
it would be hard to find someone
else that deserves it more.

